This haiku thrives on understatement. The verb *fusagu* (塞ぐ, “to block”) is concrete and tactile, grounding the poem in a specific moment. The north window (北窓) functions as a seasonal and spatial marker—it promises coolness, yet the speaker actively seals it, creating an implicit tension. The poem never explains *why* the window is blocked; reasons remain ambiguous (too much light? a draft? unwanted noise?). This ambiguity invites multiple readings, from a simple desire for darkness to a futile attempt to escape the heat.

